WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:01.723
The chain is built.

2
00:00:01.773 --> 00:00:04.940
Now it has to run, every day, for every partner.

3
00:00:04.990 --> 00:00:15.795
In this film you see what daily operation needs: monitoring, error handling, reconciliation, feedback, onboarding new partners, and moving to new schema versions.

4
00:00:17.045 --> 00:00:19.062
What does a normal day look like?

5
00:00:19.112 --> 00:00:22.069
At night, the deliveries are generated and sent.

6
00:00:22.119 --> 00:00:26.267
In the morning, files from partners arrive, are loaded and checked.

7
00:00:26.317 --> 00:00:30.088
By midday, the data is released for the systems that need it.

8
00:00:30.138 --> 00:00:35.413
And in the evening, an overview shows what went well and which issues are still open.

9
00:00:36.763 --> 00:00:37.779
First, monitoring.

10
00:00:37.829 --> 00:00:43.879
For every expected delivery, the system should know whether it arrived, when, and whether it was valid.

11
00:00:43.929 --> 00:00:48.499
Counts and file sizes compared with the day before reveal many problems early.

12
00:00:48.549 --> 00:00:54.540
Alerts go to the people who can act, and a daily overview shows the state of all partners at a glance.

13
00:00:55.890 --> 00:00:57.805
Such an overview can be simple.

14
00:00:57.855 --> 00:01:05.159
One row per expected delivery: the partner, the kind of data, when it was expected, when it arrived, and its status.

15
00:01:05.209 --> 00:01:10.485
Green when everything is fine, yellow for warnings, red for errors or missing files.

16
00:01:10.535 --> 00:01:14.225
This one table answers the first question of every morning.

17
00:01:15.575 --> 00:01:17.141
Who looks at the overview?

18
00:01:17.191 --> 00:01:24.162
Daily operation needs clear duty: who is responsible on which day, also early in the morning and during holidays.

19
00:01:24.212 --> 00:01:30.189
A runbook describes every delivery: where it comes from, what it needs, what to do when it fails.

20
00:01:30.239 --> 00:01:32.861
A list holds the contacts of every partner.

21
00:01:32.911 --> 00:01:37.832
And an escalation path says who decides when a delivery cannot be fixed in time.

22
00:01:39.182 --> 00:01:41.178
Errors fall into four classes.

23
00:01:41.228 --> 00:01:45.337
Technical errors: the transfer failed, or the file is broken.

24
00:01:45.387 --> 00:01:47.860
Schema errors: the file is not valid.

25
00:01:47.910 --> 00:01:52.156
Business errors: the file is valid, but values are implausible.

26
00:01:52.206 --> 00:01:53.725
And missing deliveries.

27
00:01:53.775 --> 00:01:59.521
Each class has its own handling and its own owner: operations, I T, or the data team.

28
00:02:00.871 --> 00:02:04.419
When an error occurs, follow the same steps every time.

29
00:02:04.469 --> 00:02:05.597
The alert arrives.

30
00:02:05.647 --> 00:02:06.382
Classify it.

31
00:02:06.432 --> 00:02:10.503
Inform the people affected, inside the company and the partner.

32
00:02:10.553 --> 00:02:13.774
Fix the cause, or ask the sender for a correction.

33
00:02:13.824 --> 00:02:14.493
Load again.

34
00:02:14.543 --> 00:02:20.512
And close the case with a short note, so that the same error is recognised faster next time.

35
00:02:21.862 --> 00:02:22.519
An example.

36
00:02:22.569 --> 00:02:30.299
At ten past seven, the reconciliation raises an alert: the weights of a portfolio add up to a hundred and twenty percent.

37
00:02:30.349 --> 00:02:35.442
At twenty past, it is classified as a business error, and the data is held back.

38
00:02:35.492 --> 00:02:39.043
At half past, the partner is told, with the document ID.

39
00:02:39.093 --> 00:02:46.244
At nine, the correction arrives as an amend delivery, and five minutes later it is loaded, checked and released.

40
00:02:47.594 --> 00:02:49.894
Reconciliation is the daily safety net.

41
00:02:49.944 --> 00:02:52.787
Do the positions add up to the total net assets?

42
00:02:52.837 --> 00:02:55.258
Do the weights reach one hundred percent?

43
00:02:55.308 --> 00:02:59.235
Did the net asset value move more than usual since the day before?

44
00:02:59.285 --> 00:03:02.429
Are the identifiers valid, with correct check digits?

45
00:03:02.479 --> 00:03:07.190
FreeXmlToolkit's data-quality checks are a good starting point for these rules.

46
00:03:08.540 --> 00:03:16.739
When something is wrong, tell the sender, quickly and precisely: which file, by its document ID, which element, and what was expected.

47
00:03:16.789 --> 00:03:19.571
The sender answers with a correction delivery.

48
00:03:19.621 --> 00:03:26.342
For feedback from distributors there is even a template of its own, the E F T, which FundsXML carries as well.

49
00:03:27.692 --> 00:03:31.662
Many problems are avoided with a clear agreement between partners.

50
00:03:31.712 --> 00:03:36.169
It says which data is delivered, when, and in which version and templates.

51
00:03:36.219 --> 00:03:43.842
It names cut-off times and contacts on both sides, and it describes how errors are reported and how corrections are delivered.

52
00:03:43.892 --> 00:03:45.365
One page is often enough.

53
00:03:46.715 --> 00:03:48.932
A new partner follows a fixed path.

54
00:03:48.982 --> 00:03:51.847
Agree on the scope: data, versions, schedule.

55
00:03:51.897 --> 00:03:55.863
Exchange test files, ideally built from the official examples.

56
00:03:55.913 --> 00:03:57.482
Both sides validate them.

57
00:03:57.532 --> 00:04:02.923
Then a parallel run of a few weeks, where the new delivery runs next to the old way.

58
00:04:02.973 --> 00:04:05.708
And only then the switch to live operation.

59
00:04:07.058 --> 00:04:09.114
The partner landscape keeps moving.

60
00:04:09.164 --> 00:04:15.009
New funds and share classes must be added to the right deliveries, closed or merged funds removed.

61
00:04:15.059 --> 00:04:18.318
A partner changes its systems and needs new test files.

62
00:04:18.368 --> 00:04:25.237
And when a partnership ends, the deliveries are stopped, the archive is kept, and both sides confirm it in writing.

63
00:04:25.287 --> 00:04:30.290
Each of these is a small change in the register, if the register is kept up to date.

64
00:04:31.640 --> 00:04:34.067
Security is part of daily operation too.

65
00:04:34.117 --> 00:04:37.659
Rotate the keys and passwords for the transfers regularly.

66
00:04:37.709 --> 00:04:41.127
Review who has access to the data, at least once a year.

67
00:04:41.177 --> 00:04:44.100
Watch for failed logins on the transfer servers.

68
00:04:44.150 --> 00:04:51.470
And test now and then that files can really be restored from the archive; an archive that cannot be read is no archive.

69
00:04:52.820 --> 00:04:54.891
New schema versions come regularly.

70
00:04:54.941 --> 00:05:00.284
Read the changelog, update the schema in your tools, and test with the official examples.

71
00:05:00.334 --> 00:05:06.526
Agree a date with your partners, switch over, and keep the old version running for a transition period.

72
00:05:06.576 --> 00:05:10.586
Because new elements are usually optional, most upgrades are small.

73
00:05:11.936 --> 00:05:17.692
Keep an archive of every file, sent and received, together with its validation result and its status.

74
00:05:17.742 --> 00:05:20.452
Keep it as long as your retention rules require.

75
00:05:20.502 --> 00:05:27.753
When a partner or an auditor asks months later what was delivered on a certain day, the document ID leads straight to the file.

76
00:05:29.103 --> 00:05:30.128
Finally, measure.

77
00:05:30.178 --> 00:05:32.404
How many deliveries arrived on time?

78
00:05:32.454 --> 00:05:34.491
How many had errors, per partner?

79
00:05:34.541 --> 00:05:36.388
How long did corrections take?

80
00:05:36.438 --> 00:05:43.912
Watched over months, these numbers show where the chain is weak, and they make the conversation with partners concrete.

81
00:05:43.962 --> 00:05:47.074
Good data quality is not a project; it is a habit.

82
00:05:48.424 --> 00:05:50.065
Operations depends on people.

83
00:05:50.115 --> 00:05:56.361
Make sure at least two people know each partner and each delivery, so that holidays and illness are no risk.

84
00:05:56.411 --> 00:05:58.576
Keep the runbooks written and current.

85
00:05:58.626 --> 00:06:03.473
And let new colleagues learn in the test environment, where a mistake costs nothing.

86
00:06:04.823 --> 00:06:17.308
Once a month, a short report for management helps: how many deliveries went out and came in, how many errors and corrections there were, which issues with partners are open, and which changes and new versions are coming.

87
00:06:17.358 --> 00:06:22.151
It shows that the chain works, and it makes the case for the time and money it needs.

88
00:06:23.501 --> 00:06:25.449
What goes wrong in the first year?

89
00:06:25.499 --> 00:06:38.377
Experience shows a familiar list: time zones around midnight, decimal separators from a server with other language settings, cash and derivatives without identifiers, and partners who change their systems without notice.

90
00:06:38.427 --> 00:06:43.254
Most of these show up in the parallel run, which is why it should never be skipped.

91
00:06:44.604 --> 00:06:47.188
To close, a checklist for production.

92
00:06:47.238 --> 00:06:48.967
Validation on both sides.

93
00:06:49.017 --> 00:06:51.245
Loading that never doubles data.

94
00:06:51.295 --> 00:06:53.025
An archive of every file.

95
00:06:53.075 --> 00:06:54.662
Monitoring with alerts.

96
00:06:54.712 --> 00:06:56.157
Reconciliation rules.

97
00:06:56.207 --> 00:06:59.502
A register of partners, versions and templates.

98
00:06:59.552 --> 00:07:01.780
And a plan for the next upgrade.

99
00:07:01.830 --> 00:07:08.827
With these in place, FundsXML runs quietly in the background, and that is exactly how it should be.

100
00:07:10.177 --> 00:07:11.237
And stay involved.

101
00:07:11.287 --> 00:07:13.704
Read the changelog of every new version.

102
00:07:13.754 --> 00:07:17.588
Report problems you find in the standard, and share what works.

103
00:07:17.638 --> 00:07:26.283
The working groups of FundsXML welcome people from operations, because they know best where the standard helps, and where it could help more.

104
00:07:27.633 --> 00:07:31.263
That was the last film of the series FundsXML in production.

105
00:07:31.313 --> 00:07:39.727
You find the complete example in the FundsXML examples repository on GitHub, and the community and the working groups on fundsxml dot org.

106
00:07:39.777 --> 00:07:40.954
Thanks for watching.
